          ],
          "source_type": "text",
          "source_sentence": "Figure 3 shows the thermogravimetric analysis of the maleyl amide polymer stabilizers at a heating rate of $1 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C / m m }$in air. The melamine amide polymer exhibits the highest thermal stability, since its decomposition starts at ${ \\approx } 2 0 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C }$and gradual degradation occurs up to $3 0 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } ;$then complete decomposition takes place up to $4 8 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } .$The thermal stability of the p-phenylenediamine polymer is lower than that of the melamine one, since the decomposition begins at ${ \\approx } 1 6 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } ,$and after $2 5 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C }$the complete degradation occurs. The least thermally stable amide polymer is that of m-phenylenediamine, as shown in Fig. 3."
        },
